This page has no mathematical derivations. It is a set of aphoristic, methodological and philosophical notes, separated by horizontal rules. They continue the previous page's list of 'one-sided principles' (Lagrange avoiding figures, von Staudt never drawing a figure) and its discussion of the Archimedean axiom.

(1) 'Math. ist die Wissenschaft, wo man immer Recht hat.' This is an aphorism on the certainty of mathematics.

(2) In mathematics it matters not only *that* something is proved but also *how* ('auf das wie?!'). This anticipates Hilbert's later concern with purity of method, already raised on the previous page with proofs with or without the Archimedean axiom.

(3) 'Rechnungsmethode = allgemeine Methode, vermöge deren sich jede Identität erzwingen lässt! daher ist die gewöhnliche Rechnung nur Spezialfall meiner axiomatischen Methode!' The claim is that calculation is a general procedure for forcing (verifying) identities. Ordinary calculation is therefore subsumed as a special case of the axiomatic method. Read in modern terms (my gloss): computing in a field or ring is deduction from the field or ring axioms, i.e. equational reasoning, so algebraic manipulation is formal derivation inside an axiom system.

(4) A polemic against 'strictly special postulates sucked from one's fingers in the study':
- Kronecker, for banishing irrational numbers;
- Holzmüller's so-called elementary method, which avoids differential and integral calculus;
- von Staudt, for avoiding all figures, although figures are 'the main language and means';
- Dedekind, who wants to name the things 'line' and 'point' quite differently, e.g. 'cat', 'love';
- Méray, who will not even draw the circle of convergence.

(5) Unity of science. We carry unity into nature by trying to master a body of facts (e.g. the geometric ones) through *one* system of axioms. In doing so we abstract, somewhat arbitrarily, from other properties of bodies (e.g. physical ones). Hence detaching a particular science such as geometry is not necessary. In truth there is only one science; everything hangs together.

(6) A historical observation. The preconceived principles of individual scholars (Kronecker's abolition of irrationals, Dedekind's renaming of concepts away from everyday usage, the sentence continuing on the next page) have repeatedly been overtaken by the actual course of science.

The next page continues with the Archimedean axiom and the completeness axiom splitting the classical continuity axiom, and with the axiomatic method as the means of bringing intuition into logical connection.
